<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      Trinix.Sandbox — the transient systemd unit an application runs inside.

      Doc 04's central claim is that launching an application *is* constructing a
      unit from the bundle's signed permissions. This assembly is that
      construction and nothing else: it takes a verified BundleInfo and a set of
      resolved paths and returns a data structure. It does not start anything,
      it does not talk to D-Bus, and it does not shell out to systemd-run.

      That split is deliberate and it is what makes the half of doc 04 that can
      be tested on a developer's Mac testable. A library whose only observable
      behaviour was "the application started" could be checked in a VM, once,
      slowly, by someone with a VM. A library that returns properties can be
      checked by asserting that a bundle with no network.client produces
      PrivateNetwork=yes — which is the actual security property, stated
      directly, in a test that runs in milliseconds.

      ⚠ The corollary is the thing this assembly cannot know: whether systemd
      accepts what it produced. See SandboxCapabilities for the properties whose
      availability depends on how systemd was built, and base/recipes/systemd/
      for how Trinix's was.

      It references Trinix.Bundle for the permission vocabulary, and nothing
      else. One vocabulary, in the assembly that already refuses an unknown one
      at seal time, is what stops the packager and the launcher from disagreeing
      about what a permission is.
    -->

    <PropertyGroup>
        <RootNamespace>Trinix.Sandbox</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>Trinix.Sandbox</AssemblyName>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Trinix.Bundle\Trinix.Bundle.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
